  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p93-q4-2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 93, NULL, 'Read the expressions of liking and disliking taken from the passage in B. First, complete the sentences. Then, arrange the sentences in order of ''liking very much'' to ''disliking very much''. (4) He loves __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p93-q5', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 93, 5, 'Read the following text about Paul''s visit to Kobita''s house and answer the following questions. Questions 5. Why do you think Paul visited Kobita''s house?'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p93-q5-2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 93, NULL, 'Read the expressions of liking and disliking taken from the passage in B. First, complete the sentences. Then, arrange the sentences in order of ''liking very much'' to ''disliking very much''. (5) He doesn''t like __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p93-q6', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 93, NULL, 'Read the expressions of liking and disliking taken from the passage in B. First, complete the sentences. Then, arrange the sentences in order of ''liking very much'' to ''disliking very much''. (6) He is afraid of __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p93-q7', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 93, NULL, 'Read the expressions of liking and disliking taken from the passage in B. First, complete the sentences. Then, arrange the sentences in order of ''liking very much'' to ''disliking very much''. (7) He hates __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p95-q1', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 95, 1, 'Now write sentences using the table about people in Paul''s family and your family. 1. Paul likes watching animals. I like watching animals too.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p95-q1-2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 95, 1, 'Use the clues to write questions and answers about likes/dislikes. 1. Mr. Jabbar works with computers. Q: (like) Does Mr. Jabbar like working with computers? A: (Yes, love) Yes, he loves working with computers.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p95-q2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 95, 2, 'Now write sentences using the table about people in Paul''s family and your family. 2. His sister hates spiders but my sister does not hate them.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p95-q2-2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 95, 2, 'Use the clues to write questions and answers about likes/dislikes. 2. Rabiul goes shopping at the weekend. Q: (like) Does Robiul like going shopping at the weekend? A: (No, hates) No, he hates going shopping at the weekend.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p95-q3', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 95, 3, 'Use the clues to write questions and answers about likes/dislikes. 3. The students watch cricket. Q: (like) __________? A: (Yes, like) __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p95-q4', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 95, 4, 'Use the clues to write questions and answers about likes/dislikes. 4. Rezina goes to the doctor. Q: (Like) __________? A: (No, not like) __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p96-q5', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 96, 5, 'Use the clues to write questions and answers about likes/dislikes. 5. Your sister watches films. Q: (Like) __________? A: (Yes, love) __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p96-q6', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 96, 6, 'Use the clues to write questions and answers about likes/dislikes. 6. We waste our time. Q: (like) __________? A: (No, hate) __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch8-p96-q7', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch8', 96, 7, 'Use the clues to write questions and answers about likes/dislikes. 7. They donate money to the poor. Q: (like) __________? A: (Yes, love) __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p100-q1', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 100, 1, 'Choose the right answer. 1. Which one of these is an example of climate change? a. a long day b. a windy day c. a hot summer day d. a sunny day'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p100-q2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 100, 2, 'Choose the right answer. 2. What does ''climbing'' mean in the statement "temperatures are climbing"? a. shifting b. going up c. being in crisis d. surviving'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p100-q3', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 100, 3, 'Choose the right answer. 3. If things go on like this... In this sentence ''go on'' means a. to go places b. to change times c. to walk fast d. to continue'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p100-q4', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 100, 4, 'Choose the right answer. 4. How did Samina and Arif react to what Anwar said about climate change? a. They showed no interest. b. They became afraid of going out alone. c. They wanted to know more about climate change. d. They agreed to attend a lecture on climate change.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p103-q1', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 103, 1, 'Tick the best answer. 1. One outcome of climate change in Bangladesh is a. summers are becoming shorter. b. summers are becoming milder. c. the monsoon is becoming irregular. d. the cities are getting larger.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p103-q2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 103, 2, 'Tick the best answer. 2. The cyclone that hit Bangladesh in 2009 is called a. Hurricane. b. Sidr. c. Aila. d. Nargis.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p103-q3', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 103, 3, 'Tick the best answer. 3. It is likely that 10.9 percent of Bangladesh will go under water if the sea level rises to a. 40 centimetres. b. 45 centimetres. c. 35 centimetres. d. 50 centimetres.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p103-q4', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 103, 4, 'Tick the best answer. 4. The phrase ''coastal region'' means an area a. with deep forests. b. of dry lands. c. with a huge population. d. close to the sea.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p103-q5', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 103, 5, 'Tick the best answer. 5. The phrase ''prolonged cold spells'' means a. long duration of cold waves. b. long gap between two winters. c. longer nights during winter. d. delay in doing something due to cold.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p104-q1', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 104, 1, 'In small groups discuss these questions. 1. Which animals in Bangladesh are in danger of extinction? What do you think we should do to protect these animals?'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p104-q2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 104, 2, 'In small groups discuss these questions. 2. What problems do you think deforestation can cause in Bangladesh?'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q1', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 1, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 1. If I have shorter showers I will B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q10', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 10, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 10. Batteries are highly toxic, if I need to use batteries I can B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 2, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 2. I can help keep cars off the road B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q3', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 3, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 3. When I leave a room I can save energy by B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q4', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 4, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 4. Putting on a jumper instead of the heater B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q5', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 5, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 5. Plastic bags harm wildlife, instead I can B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q6', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 6, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 6. I can dry my hair in the sun B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q7', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 7, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 7. I can reduce, reuse, and recycle B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q8', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 8, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 8. In my garden, I should plant B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p106-q9', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 106, 9, 'Match the sentence parts in A with those in B. 9. When I am shopping, I should B: (a) will keep me warm in winter. (b) the waste I make. (c) choose products with less packaging. (d) plants that are good for environment. (e) use rechargeable ones. (f) use bags made of jute or paper. (g) turning the light off. (h) help save water at home. (i) by walking to school and shops. (j) without using a hair dryer.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p107-q1', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 107, 1, 'Now write a few slogans for a poster using the following clues: 1. Don''t throw your wastes __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p107-q2', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 107, 2, 'Now write a few slogans for a poster using the following clues: 2. Do not use __________ bags while shopping.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p107-q3', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 107, 3, 'Now write a few slogans for a poster using the following clues: 3. You can not use too much water for __________.'),
  ('class7-eft-en-ch9-p107-q4', 'class7-eft-en', 'class7-eft-en-ch9', 107, 4, 'Now write a few slogans for a poster using the following clues: 4. People must use __________.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 1, 'Rewrite these sentences using plural nouns. One is done for you: Abus is quicker than a rickshaw. / Buses are quicker than rickshaws.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q10', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 4, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or plural noun: Do you know the ______ who lives next door? ( man/men)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q11', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 5, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or plural noun: The dentist pulled out the that was hurting. (tooth/teeth)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q12', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 6, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or plural noun: The __ who was waiting here has gone home. (boy/boys)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q13', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 1, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or a plural verb: The children ready for school. (is getting/ are getting)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q14', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 2, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or a plural verb: My uncle in the army. (is/are)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q15', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 3, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or a plural verb: The flower sweet. (smell/smells)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q16', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 4, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or a plural verb: The chocolates we bought very expensive. (was/were)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q17', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 5, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or a plural verb: The letters you posted yesterday confidential. (was/were)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q18', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 6, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or a plural verb: The young man you met yesterday transferred to this 8 office recently. (has been/ have been)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 2, 'Rewrite these sentences using plural nouns. One is done for you: A tiger is a ferocious animal. / ______'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 3, 'Rewrite these sentences using plural nouns. One is done for you: A banker earns more money than a teacher. / ______'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 4, 'Rewrite these sentences using plural nouns. One is done for you: A laptop is an expensive piece of equipment. / ______'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 5, 'Rewrite these sentences using plural nouns. One is done for you: Astudent has to work hard. / ______'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q6', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 6, 'Rewrite these sentences using plural nouns. One is done for you: Agood book helps to pass time. / ______'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q7', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 1, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or plural noun: The stops outside our house. (car / cars).'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q8', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 2, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or plural noun: The were waiting for me. (child/children)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p3-q9', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 3, 3, 'Complete these sentences with a singular or plural noun: The are in the shelf. ( book/books)'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p5-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 5, NULL, 'Now use personal pronouns in the following text: Maruf’s father is a businessman. has been in business for about 15 years. Currently in his farm, there are 20 people working full time. has three children who are going to school. Maruf is second child.The youngest and eldest are both daughters.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p5-e2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 5, NULL, 'Let us use pronouns in their possessive forms. Look at the examples : This is our school. Who is your brother? Do you know where my pen is? Look at the following sentences. ‘Yours’, ‘hers’, ‘his’ and ‘ours’ are also possessives. This is my book. That is yours. These toys are hers. This bicycle is his. This garden is ours. Now use possessives in the following sentences. After retirement Mr Kamal is living in own village. He lives with his wife who has also retired from job as a school teacher. children live in Bogra where they have own © families. children often ask them to visit them.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p6-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 6, NULL, 'Now look at the passage below and fill in the gaps with adjectives: My uncle is a ______ engineer. He works in a ______ company as a ______ engineer. He graduated in ______ engineering from BUET. He also studied abroad for his ______ qualification. Currently he is in charge of a ______ project.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p7-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 7, 1, 'Now use appropriate words in the blanks of the following sentences: My friend Tumpa is ______ in music. But she thinks cricket is an ______ game.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p7-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 7, 2, 'Now use appropriate words in the blanks of the following sentences: Moynamoti is an ______ place to visit but my uncle was ______ with the long journey there.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p7-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 7, 3, 'Now use appropriate words in the blanks of the following sentences: I felt really ______ to see my teacher in my uncle’s wedding; it was ______ too.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p7-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 7, 4, 'Now use appropriate words in the blanks of the following sentences: The film was quite ______ but I felt ______ to my favorite heroine there.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p7-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 7, 5, 'Now use appropriate words in the blanks of the following sentences: I was so ______ in the work that I could finish in due time. And my teacher thinks it is ______.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p10-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 10, NULL, 'Use the following prepositions in sentences of your own: with, on, in, to, up, over, for, at, of, by,'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1-p10-e2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch1', 10, NULL, 'Complete the following text by using appropriate prepositions. My auntie lives ______ Mogbazar just ______ Holy Family Hospital. Her flat is ______ the 5th floor. She lives ______ her family there. She often visits her good friend and neighbour who lives just one floor up; the top floor ______ the building. My auntie has been living in her flat ______ the last three years.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10-p90-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10', 90, NULL, 'Find out the possessives: Whose child is this? “Whose child is this?” | asked one day Seeing a little one out at play. “Mine”, said the parent with a tender smile © “Mine to keep a little while To bathe his hands and comb his hair To tell him what he is to wear To prepare him that he may always be good And each day do the things he should” “Whose child is this?” | asked again As the door opened and someone came in “Mine,’said the teacher with the same tender smile “Mine,” to keep just for a little while To teach him how to be gentle and kind To train and direct his dear little mind To help him live by every rule And get the best he can from school” “Whose child is this?” | asked once more Just as the little one entered the door “Ours”, said the parent and the teacher as they smiled And each took the hand of the little child “Ours to love and train together Ours this blessed task forever.” [Anonymous]'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10-p94-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10', 94, NULL, 'Identify the possessives in the following story: Two sick men were given the same room in a hospital. One man could sit up in his bed for about an hour. His bed was next to the room’s only window. The other man spent all his time lying on his bed opposite the window. The men talked for hours almost every day. They talked about their wives and children, their homes, their jobs and almost about everything. Every afternoon the man in the bed by the window would sit up and talked to his roommate about all the things he could see outside the window. The man on the other bed eagerly waited for this speaking and listening time. His friend’s vivid descriptions of passing things seen through the window inspired him to live. The window overlooked a park with a lovely lake. Ducks and swans play on the water, while children float their paper boats. A wonderful view of the skyline can be seen in the distance. The man by the window told his friend all this in detail. The listener would close his eyes and see everything in his mind’s eye.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10-p94-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10', 94, 1, 'Singular and plural possessive nouns: This is a tiger. Its body has stripes. => The tiger’s body has stripes.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10-p94-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10', 94, 2, 'Singular and plural possessive nouns: There are spiders. Their webs catch insects.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10-p94-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10', 94, 3, 'Singular and plural possessive nouns: There is a book on the table. Its pictures are obscure.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10-p94-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10', 94, 4, 'Singular and plural possessive nouns: There is a cat. Its baby is called a kitten.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10-p94-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10', 94, 5, 'Singular and plural possessive nouns: There is one shark. Its teeth are sharp.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10-p94-q6', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10', 94, 6, 'Singular and plural possessive nouns: There are sheep. Their fur keeps them warm.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10-p94-q7', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch10', 94, 7, 'Singular and plural possessive nouns: Bangladesh is a beautiful country. Its nature is green.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p95-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 95, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: What do you do in your spare time?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p95-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 95, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: Write 3 sentences about your pastime.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p96-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 96, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: What class do you read in?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p96-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 96, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: What is the name of your school?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p96-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 96, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: What is the name of your country?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p97-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 97, 1, '[Image: A black-and-white photograph of a village house with a tin roof among trees (banana and coconut palms), a cow grazing nearby, and a winding path crossing an open field in front of the house.] Look at the following picture and talk about it: <3 > hall + ae or Aspe e 7 + eg | ye. wae As Es TEE Sa ne wee rf. , wy. oo me , mie 8, i > Si me ae 7 & ot ars a vy e a } ro : ot Sit Sy Fe PX < At - « co a ZS . eat a '' te aie ¢ Ye ——"" 7 y , ° ey pom iren” VA ke i mm ee ar Ht Ls q iittala es bite ie ee - E Sik ] ‘ - : apf bene = be'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p97-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 97, 2, '[Image: A black-and-white photograph of a village house with a tin roof among trees (banana and coconut palms), a cow grazing nearby, and a winding path crossing an open field in front of the house.] Write 10 sentences about what you see in the picture.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p98-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 98, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: Can you write your name?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p98-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 98, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: Can you ride a bicycle?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p98-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 98, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: Can you swim?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p98-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 98, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: Do you have a computer at your home?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p98-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 98, NULL, 'Ask and answer the following questions: Do you like to speak English?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p100-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 100, NULL, 'Read the following sentences in Column A and match them with the negative sentences in Column B. One is done for you: Column A: 1. The Royal Bengal Tigers are available in the Sundarbans. 2. Have you ever seen any Royal Bengal Tiger? 3. You can see tigers only in the zoo. 4. The tiger is a rare animal. 5. The Royal Bengal Tiger is a beautiful animal. Column B: 1. Haven''t you ever seen any Royal Bengal Tiger? 2. Isn''t the Royal Bengal Tiger a beautiful animal? 3. The tiger is not a common animal. 4. Nowhere but in the zoo you can see tigers. 5. The Royal Bengal Tigers are not rare in the Sundarbans.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p101-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 101, NULL, 'Match the sentences on the right column with those on the left. One is done for you: Left column: a) He is an honest man. b) Lions are available in the jungles of Africa. c) The old man cannot walk fast. d) That stranger is found nowhere. e) He is the best player in the team. f) A mouse is afraid of a rat. g) Manik never tells a lie. h) Always speak the truth. i) Only a bird can fly so high. j) None but the shopkeeper was waiting there. Right column: a) The old man can walk slowly. b) The stranger is not found anywhere. c) It is not that a mouse is not afraid of a rat. d) Lions are not unavailable in jungles of Africa. e) Manik always speaks the truth. f) Only the shop keeper was waiting there. g) Is he not an honest man? h) Nothing but a bird can fly so high. i) No other player in the team is as good as he. j) Never tell a lie.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p104-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 104, NULL, 'Write a dialogue between you and your friend to get some information to write a paragraph on ‘My Friend’.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p104-e2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 104, NULL, 'Write a paragraph on ‘My Friend’.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 1, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : Ask your friend to switch on the light.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q10', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 10, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : Tell your classmates to go to the stadium to watch a cricket match.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 2, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : Ask for snacks in a restaurant.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 3, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : Advise your younger brother to be attentive in study.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 4, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : Request your mother to give something.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 5, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : Ask somebody not to do something.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q6', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 6, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : Teacher tells a student to go out of the room.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q7', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 7, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : Somebody is very angry and asking someone to do something immediately.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q8', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 8, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : Ask somebody to remember something.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p107-q9', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 107, 9, 'Read the situations below and write imperative sentences. Remember, a positive imperative begins with a main verb and a negative imperative begins with a negative auxiliary (do not / don’t) : A naughty boy is giving a threat to one of his friends.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p108-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 108, NULL, 'Read the passage below and fill in the gaps with the words in the box: Box: don''t, let''s, should, never, snatch. Rifat went to his village home in the Summer holiday. He enjoyed the time very much with his cousins there. They went to many places around the village. One of his cousins showed him a bird''s nest in a banyan tree. All of them were excited to see the nest and the baby birds. One of them ran to the tree and tried to climb on to catch the baby birds. Rifat said to him, "No, ----- do that. Don''t ----- away the kids from their mother." Nantu said, "But it is fun, ----- climb the tree and watch what the kids are doing." Rifat said, "We ----- not disturb them." Milon said, "Right, our teacher said, "----- torture the baby birds or any other animals around us for your pleasure. It is harmful for the environment."'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p108-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 108, 1, 'You went to your village to meet your relatives. You found that some village children cannot wash hands properly. Write how you will encourage them to wash their hands properly using imperative sentences.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p108-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 108, 2, 'Recall how your teachers gave instructions using imperative sentences and write at least five of them.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p108-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 108, 3, 'Does your mother/father advise/ forbid/ request you to do something using imperative sentences? Write at least 10 of them.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p108-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 108, 4, 'If/ you/ want/ a glass of water, how will you ask your younger brother/sister for it?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p111-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 111, NULL, 'Suppose you are for the first time on the beach of Cox’s Bazar. Describe how you express your wonder in exclamatory sentences.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p111-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 111, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences as exclamatory sentences: I wish I would be a King.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p111-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 111, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences as exclamatory sentences: The garden is very charming.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p111-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 111, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences as exclamatory sentences: We rejoice that our team won the match.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p111-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 111, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences as exclamatory sentences: I wish you good morning sir.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p111-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 111, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences as exclamatory sentences: The place is very dirty.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p111-q6', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 111, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences as exclamatory sentences: Let curse befall the traitors of the country.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p111-q7', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 111, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences as exclamatory sentences: This town is very peaceful.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p111-q8', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 111, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences as exclamatory sentences: I am very desirous to meet you once more.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p112-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 112, 1, 'Write how you wish your friend for coming round soon from sickness.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p112-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 112, 2, 'Write a message wishing a happy new year to any of your close friends or relatives.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p112-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 112, 3, 'Pray for the welfare of your country.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p112-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 112, 4, 'Pray for somebody to be successful in business.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11-p112-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch11', 112, 5, 'Pray for the Bangladesh cricket team to win the match and prepare three banners.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch12-p116-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch12', 116, 1, '[Image: A black-and-white photograph of a busy city street crowded with buses, cars and crowds of people, lined with multi-storey buildings carrying billboards and shop signs.] Look at the picture and describe what you see in the picture and write sentences on the structure there + to be + singular/plural subjects: 4 bh . — eric | LS Saeante i\ { = — ~ 5 See ee eee ee | Ty Mie ish peaeie- a | i t = = = Song [sill nT ihe ae sisi. Rae ae Yb ad hal isos a OP St hivS oe gt eee po hl ret TE, Oris x Sin- gee Papel s (ie aol ae oa ge One Sic oa tt eh WS a wa — LJ : Md : ead ~ ae /) ee a TAS aa an -. Te ae 4 Satay = i e > a Ra rs aa ; 5 act a - as 3 a x ae . ; = Ra) d é u ps ie / fee o> ae Le aan'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch12-p116-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch12', 116, 2, 'Now write a paragraph on‘A Street”'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch12-p117-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch12', 117, NULL, 'Make sentences with the words in the following table: There | is | a bridge over the river; There | are | a tree in front of the house; There | was | some girls on the roof; There | were | many birds in the winter season in Bangladesh; There | must be | no snakes in the garden; There | have been/has been | mosquitoes in the jungle; There | shall/will be | some problem with her; There | ______ | nobody like you; There | ______ | no denying the fact that he killed the bird; There | ______ | no storm; There | ______ | no way to come out; There | ______ | many children to go to sleep without food.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p119-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 119, 1, 'Look at the pictures and say what things are there. One is done for you: [Image: Ten numbered pictures: 1. a cat sitting on a chair; 2. a boat crowded with people; 3. a bunch of flowers in a vase; 4. a boat on water; 5. a decorated vase; 6. a plastic water bottle; 7. two birds on a tree branch; 8. a water bottle; 9. a wooden chair; 10. a tree.] What is there in picture 1? - There is a cat on the chair.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p119-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 119, 2, 'Look at the pictures and say what things are there. One is done for you: [Image: Ten numbered pictures: 1. a cat sitting on a chair; 2. a boat crowded with people; 3. a bunch of flowers in a vase; 4. a boat on water; 5. a decorated vase; 6. a plastic water bottle; 7. two birds on a tree branch; 8. a water bottle; 9. a wooden chair; 10. a tree.] Is there any water in the bottle of picture 6? - No, there isn’t any water in the bottle.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p120-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 120, NULL, 'Look at the picture of a living room. Make a list of things in the room and also say what things are not in the room. [Image: A black-and-white photograph of a living room with a white sofa set, a rug, a wooden cabinet, framed pictures on the wall, a tall plant by a glass door, and windows with dark curtains.]'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p122-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 122, 1, 'Write a dialogue using ‘there isn’t’ and ‘there aren’t’ and act it out (group work). Some clues are given in the table below: There isn’t/aren’t any computer in this room; any bad smell here; any germs in the water; any mosquitoes in this room; any dust in this room; any water in the bottle.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, 2, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you:'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q10', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There are no tourists in this season.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q11', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There is no electricity in the city.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q12', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, 3, 'Suppose,there is no tubewell for drinking water in your village. You have to go to the neighbouring village to collect drinking water. The people in the village are suffering from various kinds of diseases and problems. Describe all these problems to your friend. You can start this way: There isn’t any tubewell in our village.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There are many villages in our country. There aren’t many villages in our country.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There is a biting dog in the house.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There is an old tree beside the river.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There are no snakes in the jungle.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q6', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There is no police man in the street.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q7', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There are no beggars on the station.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q8', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There is no cleaner in the hotel.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13-p123-q9', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch13', 123, NULL, 'Rewrite the following sentences using ‘isn’t/ aren’t’. One is done for you: There is no room service in this hotel.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p125-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 125, NULL, 'Read the passage below and identify ‘to+ infinitives’ and ‘bare infinitives’: A crow was thirsty. He was flying around to find water. The day was very hot. Water was not available anywhere. Suddenly the crow found a pitcher. He thought there might be water in the pitcher. But the water was at the bottom of the pitcher. The crow was wondering how to drink the water. As he began to look around, he saw some pebbles lying on the ground nearby. Seeing this, he thought of a way to use the pebbles to reach the water at the bottom of the pitcher. Then the crow picked up the pebbles with his beak and dropped them in the pitcher to make the water rise. Soon the water level was high enough for the crow to reach. Thus he was able to drink enough water to quench his thirst.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p127-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 127, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: Eliza recommended ---------- (eat) in a low cost restaurant while we''re in Hong Kong.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: I demand ---------- (talk) to the manager of the hotel immediately.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q10', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: I began (learn) French when I was a young boy.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q11', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: We thanked him for (come) to the party.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q12', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: He keeps (lie) to everybody.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q13', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: Would you mind (open) the window please?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q14', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: John has at last given up (smoke).'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q15', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: Mary doesn''t like (be) late for school.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q16', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: (Read) novels is his favourite pastime.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q17', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: Don''t start (sing) until you are asked to.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q18', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: Do you remember (write) to him?'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q19', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Complete with right form of verbs: Jack can''t stand (ride) a bus. What kind of verbs are these? -----------------------'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: My grandmother recalled --------- (see) a plane for the very first time when she was six.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: She claims ------- (be) related to George Washington, but I don''t believe her.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: This broken bicycle needs -------- (fix) before someone can ride it.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: I can''t think of ------- (drive) such a big car when gas prices are so high, not to mention what it does to the environment.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q6', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: She managed -------- (communicate) with them, even though she didn''t speak their language.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q7', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: Don''t hesitate ------- (ask) for help if you don''t understand the directions.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q8', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: The course lasts ten days and involves --------- (hike) more than fifty miles through rugged mountainous terrain.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p128-q9', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 128, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verbs: She refused --------- (speak) to me after our fight. What kind of verbs are these? ------------------------------'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p129-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 129, 3, 'Write the past participles of the following verbs. An example is given for you: Verb / Present participle / Past participle: sleep / sleeping / slept; publish / ______ / ______; go / ______ / ______; find / ______ / ______; arrest / ______ / ______; swim / ______ / ______; develop / ______ / ______; pick / ______ / ______; fall / ______ / ______; say / ______ / ______; give / ______ / ______.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p129-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 129, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verb: This story is--------(go) to be published in the magazine.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p129-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 129, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verb: It’s -------- outside (rain).'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p129-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 129, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verb: I found the girl in the garden ------- (sleep) alone.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p129-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 129, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verb: The thief was ------ (catch) by the people.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p129-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 129, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verb: Mr. Mamun is in his living room ------ (watch) television.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14-p129-q6', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch14', 129, NULL, 'Fill in the gaps with the right form of verb: This news will be ------ (publish) in The Daily Star.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch15-p131-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch15', 131, NULL, 'Read the following passage and rewrite it using capital letters where necessary: mahmuda is an intelligent girl. she reads in class 7. the name of her school is kushumpur high school. her favourite subject is english. She has one sister, named lubna. they live in kushumpur, a village in bogra.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch15-p135-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch15', 135, NULL, 'Rewrite the following passage using capital letters and appropriate punctuation marks: rip van winkle stopped by a place where there had been a school and asked the crowd that had gathered where is schooner, the school master somebody said oh schooner he went to war in sixty three and never came back and van ammel asked rip van winkle he died eighteen years back said another voice in the crowd rip van winkle thought he was going mad had he slept all these years on the mountain finally rip van winkle asked does anyone here remember rip van winkle a very old woman said yes he was my lazy son he went up the mountains twenty one years back but never returned His dog came back without him rip van winkle was overjoyed he said mother it is me rip van winkle dont you recognize me oh my son it is really you where have you been all this while mother and son hugged each other. rip van winkle had indeed slept for twenty-one years.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p138-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 138, 1, 'Now rewrite the following in indirect speech: Maria said to her mother, ‘I have got 10 out of 10 in mathematics class test. I am very happy. My teacher became very happy. I''ll do my homework regularly. Then I’ll get good marks in every subject.’ Mom said to Maria, ‘Practice makes a man perfect’.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: I say, “I want to sleep.”'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q10', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: Meena said, “I am very busy now.”'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q2', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: He says, “I like mangoes”.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q3', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: You said, “I will meet my teacher.”'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q4', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: They said, “We are starting now.”'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q5', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: Mother said to me, “I was waiting for you for a long time.”'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q6', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: Maria says, “My father is an Engineer.”'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q7', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: He said, “I have passed the math test.”'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q8', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: She said to me, “You are my only friend.”'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p139-q9', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 139, NULL, 'Change the following sentences into indirect speeches: He said, “I have got a bad headache.'),
  ('class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16-p140-e1', 'class7-english-grammar-bn', 'class7-english-grammar-bn-ch16', 140, NULL, 'Change the following sentence into indirect speech: Robi said to Maria, “Why are you crying? What makes you so upset? Is there anything wrong with your classmates? Have you got punishment in school?” Maria said, “Why are you so anxious?”'),
